CONCEPTS=[]
def add(lecture,text):
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  x=[v.strip() for v in line.split('|')];assert len(x)==6,(lecture,line)
  term,definition,example,false,note,pages=x
  CONCEPTS.append(dict(lecture=lecture,term=term,definition=definition,example=example,false=false,note=note,pages=[int(n) for n in pages.split(',')]))
add(1, '''
Anatomical position | A standard reference posture: upright, facing forward, upper limbs at the sides with palms forward. | A teacher describes the thumb as lateral even when a model turns its hand. | Directional terms must be redefined every time a patient changes posture. | Reference terminology remains tied to anatomical position rather than the observer's changing viewpoint. | 2,3
Subject's right and left | Right and left refer to the person being described, not to an observer facing them. | A clinician facing a student points to the student's right cheek on the clinician's left. | The patient's right is always the observer's right when they face each other. | State whose body provides the reference before interpreting a diagram or examination. | 3
Superior | Toward the head or a higher part of a structure in the stated reference position. | The nose is superior to the mouth. | Superior means closer to the skin surface. | Superior-inferior describes a head-to-foot relation, not depth. | 4
Inferior | Toward the feet or a lower part of a structure in the stated reference position. | The larynx is inferior to the oral cavity. | Inferior means toward the front of the body. | A directional statement compares specified structures along a defined axis. | 5
Anterior | Toward the front of the body in anatomical position. | The trachea lies anterior to the cervical oesophagus. | Anterior means the structure closest to the examiner irrespective of posture. | The patient's anatomical reference, not examiner location, determines the term. | 6
Posterior | Toward the back of the body in anatomical position. | The pharynx lies posterior to the nasal and oral cavities. | Posterior and superficial describe the same relationship. | Back-to-front and surface-to-depth relationships are separate dimensions. | 7
Medial | Toward the median plane of the body or specified structure. | The nasal septum is medial to either nasal concha. | Medial means toward the head. | The median plane supplies the reference; medial does not mean superior. | 8
Lateral | Away from the median plane. | The cheeks are lateral to the tongue. | Lateral means closer to the attachment of a limb. | Lateral-medial and proximal-distal answer different spatial questions. | 9
Proximal | Closer to the origin or attachment of a limb or structure. | The elbow is proximal to the wrist. | Proximal means nearest the observer. | Specify the relevant origin or attachment rather than the viewer's position. | 10
Distal | Farther from the origin or attachment of a limb or structure. | The fingers are distal to the elbow. | Distal means farther from the skin surface. | Distal is not a synonym for deep. | 11
Superficial | Closer to the body surface relative to another structure. | Skin is superficial to the masseter muscle. | A superficial structure is necessarily anterior to every deeper structure. | Depth and front-back position must be described separately. | 12
Deep | Farther from the body surface relative to another structure. | The medial pterygoid lies deep to the mandibular ramus from a lateral approach. | Deep means farther from a limb's point of attachment. | Depth is relative to the relevant surface, not a proximal-distal measurement. | 13
Ipsilateral | Located or acting on the same side. | A right-sided structure is ipsilateral to another right-sided structure. | Ipsilateral describes structures on opposite sides. | Use ipsilateral and contralateral only after specifying the structures or effects compared. | 3
Contralateral | Located or acting on the opposite side. | The left cheek is contralateral to the right cheek. | Contralateral means closer to the midline on the same side. | Opposite-side relationships differ from medial-lateral position. | 3
Median plane | A vertical plane dividing the body into right and left halves. | A midline section through the nasal septum approximates a median view. | Every sagittal plane passes through the body's exact midline. | The median plane is one sagittal plane; parallel off-midline planes are also sagittal. | 3
Sagittal plane | A plane dividing a body or structure into right and left portions. | A sagittal diagram shows relationships of the nose, mouth and pharynx. | A sagittal plane divides the body into superior and inferior portions. | An image's plane must be distinguished from the direction in which it is viewed. | 3
Coronal plane | A plane dividing a body or structure into anterior and posterior portions. | A coronal nasal section displays the septum between lateral walls. | A coronal plane is the same as a transverse plane. | Coronal is also called frontal; transverse separates upper and lower portions. | 3
Transverse plane | A plane dividing a body or structure into superior and inferior portions. | A transverse section of the pharynx shows the airway and lateral recesses. | Transverse means a vertical division into front and back. | Transverse is commonly called axial or horizontal in body imaging contexts. | 3
Gliding | Translation in which one articular surface slides relative to another. | Small sliding movements occur between many carpal bones. | Gliding requires every participating joint to be a simple hinge. | Complex joints may include gliding; the sternoclavicular joint is not classified as a simple plane joint. | 14,15,16,17
Angular movement | Movement changing the angle between articulating body segments. | Bending an elbow reduces the angle between arm and forearm. | Every angular movement is rotation around the bone's own long axis. | Flexion, extension, abduction and adduction describe different angular changes. | 18
Flexion | Bending that generally decreases the angle between body segments. | A student bends the elbow to bring a cup toward the mouth. | Flexion means moving every joint in the same visible screen direction. | Describe the relevant joint and reference position, not an image's arrow direction alone. | 19,20
Extension | Straightening that generally increases the angle between body segments. | Straightening a flexed elbow increases its joint angle. | Extension is the same as moving a limb away from the body midline. | Extension reverses flexion; abduction describes movement away from a reference midline. | 21
Hyperextension | Extension beyond the anatomical reference range or position specified. | Moving the head backward beyond an upright reference illustrates cervical extension beyond neutral. | Hyperextension is necessarily a fracture diagnosis. | The term names movement or position; pathology depends on range, force and tissue context. | 22
Dorsiflexion | Ankle movement bringing the dorsum of the foot toward the anterior leg. | Raising the toes toward the shin illustrates dorsiflexion. | Dorsiflexion means turning the sole medially. | Dorsiflexion contrasts with plantarflexion, not with inversion. | 19
Plantarflexion | Ankle movement directing the foot away from the shin, as in pointing the toes. | Rising onto the toes involves plantarflexion. | Plantarflexion means turning the sole laterally. | Ankle plantarflexion differs from eversion of the foot. | 22
Abduction | Movement away from an appropriate reference midline or axis. | Moving an arm laterally away from the trunk is shoulder abduction. | Finger abduction is defined only by distance from the trunk. | Digits use their own reference axes: the middle finger for the hand and second toe for the foot. | 23,24
Adduction | Movement toward an appropriate reference midline or axis. | Returning a laterally raised arm toward the trunk is adduction. | Adduction means decreasing the elbow angle in every case. | Direction toward the reference axis distinguishes it from flexion. | 25
Circumduction | A sequence of angular movements in which the distal part traces a circular path and the segment describes a cone. | A bowler's shoulder can combine flexion, abduction, extension and adduction during a circular motion. | Circumduction is identical to pure rotation around a bone's long axis. | A circular distal path need not be pure axial rotation. | 26,27
Rotation | Turning a body segment around its longitudinal axis. | Turning the head to look toward one side includes cervical rotation. | Every circular path of the hand must be pure rotation at one joint. | Rotation and circumduction can coexist but are not synonymous. | 28,29
Medial rotation | Rotation bringing the anterior surface of a limb toward the median plane. | With the elbow positioned appropriately, the humerus rotates inward at the shoulder. | Medial rotation necessarily moves the entire arm toward the trunk. | Axial turning differs from adduction; joint position determines the visible path. | 29
Lateral rotation | Rotation turning the anterior surface of a limb away from the median plane. | A shoulder exercise turns the humerus outward without requiring shoulder abduction. | Lateral rotation and lateral translation are identical movements. | Turning about an axis is distinct from sliding a segment sideways. | 29
Supination | Forearm rotation placing the palm anteriorly in anatomical position, with radius and ulna approximately parallel. | With the elbow flexed, a student turns the palm upward to hold a bowl. | Supination requires the radius to cross anteriorly over the ulna. | Palm-up is a useful flexed-elbow example; anatomical terminology still uses the standard reference. | 30,31,32
Pronation | Forearm rotation in which the radius crosses over the ulna and the palm turns posteriorly in anatomical position. | With the elbow flexed, the palm turns downward over a desk. | Pronation is simply flexion at the wrist. | Forearm rotation occurs at radioulnar joints rather than being a wrist-bending movement. | 30,31,32
Inversion | Turning the sole of the foot medially. | A model demonstrates the sole facing toward the other foot. | Inversion means pointing the toes downward without changing sole direction. | Most inversion occurs through hindfoot/intertarsal arrangements, not a pure ankle hinge action. | 33,34
Eversion | Turning the sole of the foot laterally. | A demonstrator turns the sole away from the body's midline. | Eversion means drawing the dorsum toward the shin. | Distinguish sole orientation from dorsiflexion at the ankle. | 33,34
Protraction | Anterior movement of a part such as the mandible. | Bringing the lower jaw forward illustrates mandibular protraction. | Protraction means closing the jaw by elevation. | Forward translation and upward movement describe different components. | 35,36
Retraction | Posterior movement of a previously protracted part. | Returning a protruded mandible backward illustrates retraction. | Retraction is synonymous with depression of the mandible. | Retraction changes anterior-posterior position; depression opens the jaw. | 35,36
Elevation | Superior movement of a body part. | Closing the mouth includes elevation of the mandible. | Elevation means moving the jaw forward. | A movement can combine superior and anterior components, which should be named separately. | 37,38
Depression | Inferior movement of a body part. | Opening the mouth includes depression of the mandible. | Depression is the same as posterior movement in anatomical description. | Anatomical depression is a movement term, not a psychological diagnosis. | 37,38
''')
add(2, '''
External nose | The projecting nasal structure enclosing the anterior entrances to the nasal cavities. | A model separates the visible nose from the deeper nasal cavity. | External nose and nasal cavity are identical anatomical regions. | The external nose has bony and cartilaginous support; the cavity extends posteriorly toward the nasopharynx. | 2,3,4
Nares | The anterior nasal openings or nostrils. | Air enters a nasal cavity through its anterior naris. | The nares open directly into the middle ear. | Posterior nasal apertures are the choanae, not the anterior nares. | 3,4
Ala nasi | The rounded lateral wing bordering a nostril. | The mobile side of a nostril is identified on an external-nose image. | The ala nasi forms the posterior bony septum. | The ala is a lateral external feature; the septum is the medial partition. | 3,4,6
Nasal dorsum and apex | The external nasal bridge/ridge and its projecting tip, respectively. | A student traces the dorsum toward the apex on a face diagram. | The apex of the nose is the posterior opening into the pharynx. | Surface labels must be distinguished from deep cavity boundaries. | 4
Bony nasal framework | Superior external support formed principally by nasal bones and adjacent frontal and maxillary components. | A diagram shows nasal bones and frontal processes of the maxillae above the cartilage. | The entire external nose is supported only by one soft cartilage. | Bone predominates superiorly while cartilage supports much of the anterior/inferior framework. | 5,6
Nasal cartilages | Cartilaginous components supporting the flexible external nose and anterior septum. | Greater alar and lateral cartilages are identified below nasal bones. | All nasal cartilages are parts of the ethmoid bone. | Cartilage and bone form related but distinct components. | 5,6
Nasal vestibule | The anterior entrance region immediately inside the nostril, initially lined by skin. | Coarse nasal hairs are seen near the entrance rather than high in the olfactory region. | The vestibule is the olfactory area beneath the cribriform plate. | The vestibule transitions into respiratory mucosa farther within the cavity. | 3,9,23
Nasal cavity floor | The hard-palate partition, formed chiefly by palatine processes of maxillae and horizontal plates of palatine bones. | A sagittal model shows the same bony partition separating mouth below from nose above. | The cribriform plate forms the nasal floor. | The hard palate is floor of the nasal cavity and roof of the oral cavity. | 9,10
Nasal cavity roof | A narrow superior boundary including frontonasal, ethmoidal and sphenoidal parts. | A sagittal view identifies cribriform plate centrally and sphenoid posteriorly. | One maxillary palatine process forms the entire nasal roof. | Different roof regions involve different bones and clinical relationships. | 11
Cribriform plate | A perforated ethmoid plate transmitting olfactory nerve filaments. | A skull model traces smell fibers upward toward the olfactory bulb. | The cribriform plate normally transmits the main mandibular nerve trunk. | It separates superior nasal structures from the anterior cranial fossa; injury can disrupt smell or permit CSF leakage. | 11,21
Nasal septum | The medial partition separating right and left nasal cavities. | A coronal view places the septum between the two sets of conchae. | The septum is the same structure as a lateral nasal concha. | Principal components are septal cartilage, perpendicular ethmoid plate and vomer with supporting crests. | 12,13
Perpendicular plate of ethmoid | The superior bony component of the nasal septum. | A medial-wall diagram places it above the vomer. | The perpendicular ethmoid plate is the movable nasal tip cartilage. | Do not confuse the vertical septal plate with the horizontal cribriform plate. | 12,13
Vomer | A bone forming an important posteroinferior part of the nasal septum. | The vomer is identified below the perpendicular ethmoid plate in a septal diagram. | The vomer is one of the three lateral nasal conchae. | Septal bones and lateral-wall projections occupy different walls. | 12,13
Septal cartilage | The main cartilaginous anterior component of the nasal septum. | A diagram shows cartilage anterior to the ethmoid plate and vomer. | Septal cartilage makes up the posterior wall of the sphenoid sinus. | Flexible anterior support contrasts with the posterior bony partition. | 12,13
Nasal conchae | Curved projections of the lateral nasal walls that increase mucosal surface and shape airflow. | Superior, middle and inferior conchae are identified above their respective meatuses. | Conchae are openings through which all sinuses drain directly. | Conchae are structures; meatuses are spaces beneath them. | 14,15
Inferior nasal concha | An independent bone forming the lowest main lateral nasal projection. | A skull image identifies it separately from the ethmoid's middle and superior conchae. | The inferior concha is always part of the ethmoid bone. | The superior and middle conchae belong to ethmoid; the inferior concha is a separate bone. | 14,15
Nasal meatus | A passage lying inferior to a nasal concha. | The inferior meatus is located beneath the inferior concha. | A meatus is the same thing as the concha above it. | Name the passage and adjacent structure separately when tracing drainage. | 14,16,17
Inferior meatus drainage | The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior nasal meatus. | Tears can reach the nose through this pathway. | The maxillary sinus normally opens into the inferior meatus. | Nasolacrimal drainage differs from the major paranasal sinus openings. | 16,17
Middle meatus drainage | The middle meatus receives frontal, maxillary and anterior/middle ethmoidal drainage through their pathways. | A student groups these drainage routes beneath the middle concha. | All posterior ethmoidal cells drain into the middle meatus. | Posterior ethmoidal drainage belongs chiefly to the superior meatus. | 16,17,32
Superior meatus drainage | Posterior ethmoidal air cells open into the superior meatus. | A drainage diagram places posterior ethmoid openings below the superior concha. | The superior meatus is the usual opening of the nasolacrimal duct. | Inferior, middle and superior passages have distinct principal openings. | 16,17,31
Sphenoethmoidal recess | A space above/posterior to the superior concha receiving the sphenoid sinus opening. | A student traces sphenoid drainage above the superior meatus. | The sphenoid sinus normally drains through the inferior meatus. | The recess is not simply another name for the superior meatus. | 16,17,30
Choanae | The paired posterior nasal apertures communicating with the nasopharynx. | Air passes posteriorly from a nasal cavity into the nasopharynx through a choana. | Choanae are the external nostril margins. | Distinguish anterior nares from posterior apertures. | 9,17
Respiratory nasal mucosa | Vascular mucosa with a mucociliary apparatus supporting air conditioning and clearance. | Inhaled air encounters moist mucosal surfaces over the conchae. | The nasal cavity contributes nothing to warming or humidifying inspired air. | Respiratory lining differs from specialized olfactory mucosa near the roof. | 18,21,27
Olfactory innervation | Smell receptors send CN I filaments through the cribriform plate toward olfactory bulbs. | A student distinguishes smelling a scent from sensing nasal irritation. | CN V is the primary special-sense nerve for smell. | Trigeminal pathways provide much general sensation, not the olfactory special sense. | 21,22
Nasal general sensation | General sensory supply is distributed through ophthalmic V1 and maxillary V2 trigeminal branches. | Touch/irritation pathways are traced separately from CN I smell fibers. | All nasal touch sensation is carried by CN I. | Specify sensory modality before choosing a nerve. | 8,21,22
External nasal sensation | V1 branches and the infraorbital nerve from V2 supply territories of external nasal skin. | A diagram distinguishes external nasal and infraorbital sensory branches. | CN VII supplies ordinary skin sensation over the whole external nose. | Facial motor supply and trigeminal skin sensation should not be conflated. | 8
Sphenopalatine artery | A terminal maxillary artery branch entering nasal regions through the sphenopalatine foramen. | Posterior nasal branches are traced from an external-carotid pathway. | The sphenopalatine artery is a branch of the internal carotid's ophthalmic artery. | It belongs to the external carotid-maxillary route, unlike ethmoidal arteries. | 18,19,20
Ethmoidal arteries | Anterior and posterior ethmoidal branches arise from the ophthalmic arterial system. | A vascular map connects nasal ethmoidal supply with an internal-carotid route. | Ethmoidal nasal arteries are branches of the facial artery. | Nasal supply links internal- and external-carotid systems through anastomoses. | 18,19,20
External nose blood supply | Facial and ophthalmic-related branches contribute substantially, with regional anastomoses. | A student traces angular/lateral nasal and dorsal nasal contributions rather than one exclusive vessel. | One artery supplies every external nasal structure without collateral routes. | The handout's abbreviated list should not be interpreted as excluding important facial branches. | 7
Nasal venous drainage | Veins form communicating networks draining toward facial, ophthalmic and deeper venous pathways. | A diagram follows veins beyond a single arterial territory. | Venous drainage is identical to lymphatic drainage into lymph nodes. | Venous and lymphatic systems are distinct despite related regional anatomy. | 18
Nasal lymph drainage | Vestibular regions commonly drain toward submandibular nodes; deeper regions communicate with retropharyngeal/deep cervical pathways. | A student separates drainage of the anterior vestibule from posterior nasal regions. | All nasal lymph must drain first to the same axillary node. | Drainage territories are regional and can overlap; they are not arterial branch lists. | 23
Paranasal sinuses | Air-containing spaces in frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid and maxillary bones communicating with the nasal cavities. | Four named groups are located around a nasal cavity on a skull model. | Paranasal sinuses are closed spaces with no normal communication to the nose. | Their small openings and mucosal lining are important for ventilation and clearance. | 25,26
Mucociliary sinus clearance | Coordinated ciliary activity moves secretions toward natural drainage pathways. | Secretions leave a maxillary sinus through its natural ostium despite its relatively high opening. | Sinus clearance normally depends entirely on blowing the nose to create suction. | Ciliary transport is central; gravity or a claimed siphon is not an adequate sole explanation. | 27,28
Sinus functions | Proposed roles include reducing skull mass and influencing the acoustic/airway environment. | A student distinguishes a nasal/sinus resonating space from the vocal-fold sound source. | Paranasal sinuses are the structures that generate normal voiced sound. | Phonation originates chiefly at vibrating vocal folds; the contribution of individual sinuses to resonance is limited/variable. | 27
Maxillary sinus | A generally pyramidal air space within the maxilla, related superiorly to the orbit and inferiorly to upper posterior teeth. | Dental and sinus symptoms are considered anatomically related without equating every toothache with sinus disease. | The maxillary sinus lies within the body of the sphenoid. | Relationships to the orbit and tooth roots explain possible clinical connections, not a diagnosis from location alone. | 28
Maxillary sinus ostium | The natural maxillary sinus opening is high on its medial wall and drains to the middle meatus. | A student explains why clearance cannot be understood as gravity alone. | The normal maxillary ostium lies in the inferior meatus at the sinus floor. | Distinguish the high natural opening from other accessory or surgical openings. | 28,32
Frontal sinus | A variably developed sinus within the frontal bone draining toward the middle-meatus pathway. | Paired frontal spaces are compared without assuming perfect symmetry. | The two frontal sinuses must be identical in every individual. | Size, asymmetry and even presence vary; fixed surface outlines are approximations. | 29
Sphenoid sinus | A sinus in the sphenoid body with important neighboring skull-base structures. | A sagittal model places it below the sellar region. | The sphenoid sinus is inside the maxillary alveolar process. | Its opening is in the sphenoethmoidal recess and nearby relationships are clinically important. | 30
Ethmoidal air cells | Multiple air cells between nasal cavity and orbit, grouped as anterior, middle and posterior. | A diagram traces different cell groups to middle or superior meatus. | Every ethmoidal cell drains through a single inferior-meatus opening. | The thin lamina papyracea separates many ethmoid cells from the orbit. | 31
Ethmoid-orbit relationship | A thin medial orbital wall lies adjacent to ethmoidal air cells. | Orbital complications of sinus infection are understood through this close relationship. | A thick isolated wall makes communication of disease anatomically impossible. | Proximity explains potential spread; symptoms require clinical assessment rather than diagram-based diagnosis. | 31,33
Sinusitis | Inflammation involving a paranasal sinus and its mucosa, often associated with impaired drainage. | An educational case connects swollen mucosa with obstructed natural ostia. | Every episode of nasal congestion proves bacterial sinus infection. | Anatomy supports mechanisms; diagnosis and treatment depend on clinical criteria. | 33
Cribriform injury | Injury near the anterior skull base can affect smell pathways or create communication for CSF leakage. | Clear nasal fluid after significant head trauma is treated as a concern requiring professional evaluation. | Every clear runny nose proves a skull-base fracture. | A serious possibility in the right context is not a diagnosis from fluid appearance alone. | 11,33
Waldeyer's ring | A ring-like arrangement of pharyngeal, tubal, palatine and lingual lymphoid tissues. | A learner locates adenoids superiorly and lingual tonsils near the tongue base. | Waldeyer's ring is formed by the three nasal conchae. | Core tonsillar groups should be distinguished from additional lateral/posterior lymphoid tissue. | 34,35
Tubal tonsil | Lymphoid tissue associated with the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube. | A nasopharyngeal diagram relates it to the torus tubarius. | Tubal tonsil and the fossa of Rosenmuller are identical structures. | The pharyngeal recess lies posterior to the tubal elevation; adjacent structures are not synonyms. | 34,35
''')
add(3, '''
Septal support | Septal cartilage and bony components rest on supporting maxillary/palatine crests and related structures. | A medial-wall image identifies cartilage, vomer, perpendicular ethmoid plate and supporting crests. | The septum consists only of a single lateral-wall concha. | The main components and supporting attachments should be distinguished. | 5,6
Nasopalatine nerve | A V2 sensory branch traversing posterior septal regions and passing toward the incisive canal. | A student traces it along the septum toward the anterior hard palate. | The nasopalatine nerve is the main motor nerve to the tongue. | Sensory septal/palatal pathways differ from hypoglossal motor supply. | 4
Anterior ethmoidal nerve | A nasociliary V1 branch contributing sensation to anterosuperior nasal regions. | An innervation diagram links it to ophthalmic rather than maxillary trigeminal territory. | The anterior ethmoidal nerve is the olfactory nerve. | General sensation and special olfaction follow different routes. | 4
Ethmoidal bulla | An elevation on the lateral wall related to middle ethmoidal air cells. | Middle-meatus anatomy is identified relative to this raised region. | The bulla is a septal cartilage in the anterior nostril. | Locate it on the lateral wall rather than the medial septum. | 2,3
Hiatus semilunaris | A curved cleft of the middle-meatus region between the bulla and uncinate process. | A diagram uses the cleft to orient sinus-drainage pathways. | The hiatus semilunaris is the oesophageal opening through the diaphragm. | Similar descriptive terms must be tied to the correct organ and compartment. | 3
Uncinate process | A thin curved ethmoidal process contributing to lateral nasal-wall and drainage anatomy. | A student locates it near the semilunar hiatus rather than in the septal midline. | The uncinate process is the mandibular condyle. | Position and neighboring spaces help identify a structure when shapes appear similar. | 2,3
Little's area | The anteroinferior septal region associated with Kiesselbach's arterial anastomosis. | A common anterior nosebleed site is located on the septum rather than the posterior pharynx. | Little's area is the same location as the sphenoid sinus opening. | Anterior septal localization distinguishes it from posterior nasal sources. | 7,8,9
Kiesselbach's plexus | An anterior septal arterial anastomotic region involving branches from both carotid systems. | A student connects anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial contributions. | Kiesselbach's plexus is a single vein draining the tongue. | Lists vary in detail, but the key is anterior septal arterial anastomosis, not a solitary vessel. | 7,8
Superior labial septal branch | A facial-artery contribution to anterior septal blood supply. | A vascular map connects a septal branch to the facial rather than ophthalmic artery. | Every superior labial branch comes from the lingual artery. | Follow the parent vessel instead of inferring it from the nearby tongue. | 7,8
Greater palatine contribution | A maxillary-system route that can reach anterior septal anastomoses via palatal/incisive pathways. | The anterior septum is connected with a palatal arterial source. | Palatal arteries have no anatomical connection with septal anastomoses. | Regional anastomoses explain why several arterial sources matter. | 7,8
Anterior epistaxis | Nasal bleeding arising from anterior regions, commonly the anterior septum. | A learner identifies an anterior septal source on a labeled diagram. | Every nosebleed emerging from a nostril must arise anteriorly. | Exit route does not alone establish the bleeding site; location requires assessment. | 8,9
Posterior epistaxis | Bleeding from deeper/posterior nasal sources, often involving sphenopalatine arterial branches. | Blood tracking into the pharynx raises concern for a posterior source in an educational case. | Blood in the throat definitively proves one posterior artery is bleeding. | Anterior bleeding can also track backward; localization and severity require clinical evaluation. | 8,9
Woodruff's plexus | A traditionally described venous plexus on the posterior lateral nasal wall near the inferior-meatus region. | A student separates the historical venous label from posterior arterial bleeding pathways. | Woodruff's plexus is simply another name for every posterior sphenopalatine artery branch. | The handout's arterial depiction is oversimplified; posterior epistaxis is often arterial and not explained by one venous label. | 8,9
Local epistaxis factors | Conditions at the nasal mucosa or adjacent structures that can predispose to bleeding. | Dryness, digital trauma or inflamed mucosa are considered local contributors. | A local cause can be identified from age alone. | Local and systemic factors can coexist; age associations do not provide a diagnosis. | 10,11,12,13,14
Inflamed nasal mucosa | Mucosal inflammation can increase friability and contribute to bleeding. | An educational case relates repeated rubbing of inflamed nasal tissue to injury. | Inflamed mucosa must always indicate a malignant lesion. | Inflammation has many possible causes and is not synonymous with neoplasia. | 10
Traumatic epistaxis | Nasal bleeding associated with local injury. | Nose picking or instrumentation may injure vulnerable mucosa. | Trauma-related bleeding excludes any contribution from anticoagulation. | A local trigger and impaired hemostasis may contribute together. | 11
Iatrogenic nasal injury | Injury arising in association with healthcare procedures or instrumentation. | Nasal instrumentation is recognized as a possible bleeding trigger. | Iatrogenic means a condition inherited from both parents. | The term describes a relationship to medical intervention, not genetic inheritance. | 11,12
Mucosal dryness | Reduced moisture can favor crusting and mucosal injury. | Dry indoor air is considered alongside local irritation in a hypothetical winter example. | Dryness is proof that the person has a clotting-factor deficiency. | A local predisposing factor is not a diagnosis of systemic coagulopathy. | 13
Septal deviation | Departure of the septal partition from the midline, potentially affecting airflow and local conditions. | A diagram relates altered airflow to focal dryness without diagnosing obstruction from shape alone. | Every septal deviation requires the same surgical treatment. | Clinical importance depends on symptoms, examination and individual context. | 13
Septal perforation | A defect through the nasal septum connecting the two sides. | A model distinguishes a hole through the septum from simple deviation. | Perforation and deviation are interchangeable terms. | Causes and consequences vary; the structural descriptions are distinct. | 14
Foreign body and nasal bleeding | A retained nasal object can irritate tissue and contribute to local symptoms. | Unilateral symptoms in a child prompt appropriate assessment for a possible foreign body. | A child's age alone establishes the presence of a foreign body. | A possibility suggested by context is not confirmation from one demographic detail. | 14,19
Neoplasm as a differential | A lesion can be considered among causes of recurrent or unusual nasal bleeding. | Persistent unilateral symptoms lead to evaluation rather than an age-based conclusion. | Middle age and a nosebleed are sufficient to diagnose a tumor. | The lecture's age associations are not diagnostic rules. | 13,19
Systemic bleeding contributors | Disorders or medications affecting vessels, platelets or coagulation can modify bleeding. | A clinician reviews bleeding history and relevant medicines alongside the local examination. | All systemic contributors are deficiencies of the same clotting factor. | Vascular abnormalities, platelet problems and factor defects are different mechanisms. | 15,16,17,18
Hypertension and epistaxis | High blood pressure can coexist with epistaxis and complicate evaluation, without proving the sole immediate cause. | An elevated reading during a stressful bleed is interpreted with history and clinical assessment. | One elevated reading proves that pressure alone caused the nosebleed. | Association and management relevance must be distinguished from a simple causal claim. | 16,19,22
Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia | An inherited vascular disorder characterized by telangiectasias/arteriovenous malformations and possible recurrent bleeding. | Recurrent epistaxis with characteristic vascular findings and family history raises an educational differential. | HHT is defined as an isolated deficiency of platelet number. | Structural vascular abnormalities differ from thrombocytopenia; diagnosis requires appropriate criteria. | 16
Granulomatosis with polyangiitis | A systemic vasculitic disorder that may involve upper-airway tissues; formerly called Wegener's granulomatosis. | A learner updates terminology in the systemic-inflammatory differential list. | Every episode of ordinary allergic rhinitis establishes GPA. | The modern name identifies the condition without turning nonspecific nasal symptoms into diagnosis. | 17
Platelet versus coagulation problems | Platelet number/function and clotting-factor pathways are distinguishable contributors to hemostasis. | Thrombocytopenia is compared with hemophilia rather than treated as the same defect. | All bleeding disorders are identical reductions in platelet count. | Uremia, liver disease, hematological disease and medicines can affect different mechanisms. | 18
Anticoagulant-related bleeding | Drugs that alter coagulation can contribute to bleeding severity or persistence. | A medication history records anticoagulant use for the treating clinician. | A learner should independently stop every anticoagulant whenever epistaxis occurs. | Medication changes require appropriate clinical decision-making; anatomy teaching is not an individual prescription. | 18,20
Initial epistaxis priorities | Assessment addresses airway, breathing, circulation, severity and relevant history before selecting treatment. | A teaching case with heavy bleeding and faintness prioritizes urgent medical assessment. | Ongoing severe bleeding should always wait seventy-two hours before escalation. | The slide's timing is not a safe universal waiting rule; clinical severity governs urgency. | 20,28
Nosebleed first-aid principle | Sitting forward and applying firm sustained pressure to the soft lower nose are common first-aid measures. | A first-aid diagram contrasts soft-part compression with squeezing only the bony bridge. | Tilting the head backward is the preferred way to keep blood out of the throat. | Professional guidance recommends forward posture; persistent/heavy bleeding or concerning symptoms need medical care. | 20,21
Nasal cautery | Clinician-applied treatment to an appropriately identified bleeding site using a suitable method. | A learner recognizes silver nitrate as a professional treatment category in the handout. | Cautery is simply another term for measuring blood pressure. | This course identifies treatment principles and anatomy, not a do-it-yourself procedural protocol. | 21,22
Anterior nasal packing | Clinician-selected material or device providing pressure for selected anterior bleeding situations. | A diagram distinguishes anterior packing from a posterior balloon arrangement. | An anterior pack guarantees control of every posterior bleed. | Site, severity and response determine escalation; no pack is universally effective. | 22,23,24
Posterior packing and monitoring | Posterior nasal packing can require hospital observation and cardiorespiratory monitoring. | A teaching plan recognizes airway and systemic concerns rather than treating packing as a trivial home measure. | Every posterior pack has identical medication and monitoring requirements for every person. | Admission, antibiotics, oxygen and other measures are selected clinically; blanket prescriptions are inappropriate. | 23,25,26
Escalation for persistent bleeding | Uncontrolled or recurrent bleeding can require specialist endoscopic treatment, arterial ligation or embolization after assessment. | A case asks why persistent severe bleeding needs escalation rather than an arbitrary waiting interval. | Failure of one treatment means anatomy is irrelevant to further management. | Vascular localization and patient condition guide specialist decisions. | 28
Prevention and follow-up | Advice addresses mucosal care, avoidance of renewed trauma and clear instructions for recurrent or severe symptoms. | A learner explains why repeated picking or forceful blowing can disturb recently controlled bleeding. | A generic discharge list replaces individual instructions and safety advice. | Clinical follow-up, medicines and care plans depend on the person's condition and local guidance. | 27
''')
add(4, '''
Temporomandibular joint | A paired synovial articulation between the mandibular condyle and temporal articular region with an intervening disc. | A model locates the joint anterior to the external acoustic meatus. | The TMJ articulates the maxilla directly with the hyoid. | Both sides function together during ordinary mandibular movements. | 2,3,4
TMJ articular surfaces | The mandibular condyle articulates with the mandibular fossa/articular eminence region of the temporal bone. | The condyle moves relative to the temporal articular region during opening. | The mandibular coronoid process is the usual TMJ articular head. | The coronoid process receives temporalis attachment; it is not the condyle. | 3,4,5
Modified hinge classification | The TMJ combines hinge-like rotation and gliding translation. | Early opening and wider opening show different dominant movement components. | The TMJ is a simple hinge with no translation. | The slide's condylar label does not capture all functional movement; ginglymoarthrodial is a common functional description. | 4,5,6
TMJ surface covering | TMJ articular surfaces are conventionally described as covered by fibrous/fibrocartilaginous tissue rather than typical hyaline cartilage. | A learner identifies this as a contrast with many other synovial joints. | Every synovial articular surface must have the same hyaline cartilage covering. | Descriptions vary in histological detail, but the basic teaching contrast is important. | 4,6
Articular disc | A fibrous/fibrocartilaginous disc dividing the TMJ into superior and inferior synovial compartments. | A sagittal view places the disc between condyle and temporal surface. | The disc divides the joint into medial and lateral nasal cavities. | The disc is typically biconcave, with a thinner intermediate region. | 5,6,7
Inferior TMJ compartment | The space between mandibular condyle and disc, associated mainly with rotation. | A diagram locates hinge-like movement below the disc. | Translation is the only movement possible below the disc. | Rotation and translation are coordinated rather than perfectly isolated in normal function. | 6,21
Superior TMJ compartment | The space between disc and temporal surface, associated mainly with translation. | The disc-condyle complex moves forward along the articular eminence during wider opening. | All jaw translation occurs between the tongue and hyoid. | Specify the joint compartment rather than inferring movement from the jaw's outline alone. | 6,21
TMJ capsule | A fibrous envelope enclosing the joint and related synovial spaces. | A diagram follows attachments around the temporal articular margins and mandibular neck. | The capsule attaches only to the tooth crowns. | Capsular attachments and disc attachments are related but not identical structures. | 6,7,8
Lateral temporomandibular ligament | A reinforcement of the lateral capsule helping constrain excessive displacement. | A lateral view identifies a thickened stabilizing band over the joint. | The lateral ligament is a branch of the facial nerve. | Ligaments provide mechanical restraint; nerves conduct signals. | 8,9
Sphenomandibular ligament | A ligament extending from the sphenoid spine toward the mandibular lingula. | A medial TMJ image locates it near the mandibular foramen region. | It runs from the styloid process to the tongue dorsum. | The attachment pair distinguishes it from stylomandibular and styloglossal structures. | 8,9,12
Stylomandibular ligament | A ligament from the styloid region toward the posterior mandibular angle/ramus region. | A diagram contrasts it with the sphenomandibular ligament. | Stylomandibular and sphenomandibular ligaments have identical attachments. | Similar names do not make separate ligaments interchangeable. | 8,9
TMJ posterior relations | The external acoustic meatus and related posterior tissues lie close to the joint. | A model explains why jaw and nearby ear-region symptoms may need careful localization. | The TMJ lies behind the external acoustic meatus. | Anatomical proximity alone does not diagnose the cause of pain. | 2,10
TMJ medial relations | Important nearby structures include the maxillary artery, auriculotemporal nerve and accessory ligaments in variable relationships. | A medial image identifies vessels and nerves near the mandibular neck. | The only medial relation is the skin over the cheek. | Nearby structures matter for localization, but variations should not be ignored. | 11,12
Auriculotemporal nerve | A V3 sensory branch contributing substantially to TMJ innervation. | Joint sensation is traced toward the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. | The auriculotemporal nerve is a branch of the hypoglossal nerve. | TMJ sensory and tongue motor pathways have different parent cranial nerves. | 12,13
TMJ additional innervation | Masseteric and deep temporal V3-related branches can contribute to joint sensory supply. | A learner avoids assigning all joint sensation exclusively to one small branch. | TMJ sensory supply comes exclusively from CN I. | Auriculotemporal is prominent, but several relevant branches may contribute. | 13
TMJ arterial supply | Branches associated with superficial temporal and maxillary arteries contribute to joint vascular supply. | A student places TMJ vascular supply within external-carotid pathways. | The TMJ is normally avascular because it is a synovial joint. | Vascular tissues surrounding articular regions differ from the poorly vascular central disc. | 12,14
Muscles of mastication | Masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid and lateral pterygoid are the four principal paired masticatory muscles. | A student groups the four before adding assisting suprahyoid muscles separately. | Every facial-expression muscle is one of the four principal masticatory muscles. | A functional assistant is not necessarily a member of the anatomical four-muscle group. | 14,15,16,17,18
Mastication motor supply | Motor branches of the mandibular division of CN V supply the principal masticatory muscles. | Masseteric and deep temporal nerves are traced to V3. | All four principal masticatory muscles receive motor supply from CN VII. | Facial-expression and principal mastication motor pathways differ. | 13,14
Masseter attachments | Masseter runs principally from the zygomatic arch to the lateral mandibular ramus and angle. | A lateral diagram shows its broad external mandibular attachment. | The masseter originates on the tongue's median septum. | Attachment position helps explain its strong elevating action. | 15
Masseter action | Masseter primarily elevates the mandible; portions can assist other components such as protrusion. | A learner identifies masseter activity during jaw closure. | Masseter is the principal muscle that protrudes the tongue. | Jaw and tongue movements involve different structures despite close location. | 14,15,19
Temporalis attachments | Temporalis arises from the temporal fossa/fascia and inserts onto the coronoid process and anterior ramus. | A fan-shaped muscle is traced beneath the zygomatic arch toward the coronoid process. | Temporalis inserts principally onto the mandibular condyle's articular surface. | Coronoid attachment distinguishes it from the joint's articular head. | 16
Temporalis action | Temporalis elevates the mandible; posterior fibers contribute importantly to retraction. | A protruded jaw is brought backward with posterior temporalis contribution. | Every temporalis fiber acts only to depress the jaw. | Different fiber directions contribute differently to the movement. | 14,16,19
Lateral pterygoid heads | Superior and inferior heads attach from sphenoid-related regions to the disc/capsule and condylar-neck region. | A diagram differentiates upper disc/capsule association from lower condylar association. | The two heads are intrinsic tongue muscles with no joint association. | Precise attachment contributions vary; avoid treating both heads as identical through every movement. | 3,17
Lateral pterygoid action | Coordinated activity assists protrusion, translation/opening and side-to-side movement. | The condyle-disc complex translates during mouth opening. | The lateral pterygoid acts solely as a jaw elevator. | Head-specific activation is complex; simple action lists are introductory summaries. | 14,17,19,21
Medial pterygoid attachments | Medial pterygoid extends from pterygoid/maxillary regions to the medial mandibular ramus and angle. | A diagram contrasts its medial insertion with masseter's lateral insertion. | Medial pterygoid and masseter insert on the same external surface only. | Together they form a functional muscular sling around the mandibular angle. | 18
Medial pterygoid action | Medial pterygoid elevates and assists protrusion and side-to-side mandibular movement. | Its action is considered with masseter during chewing. | Medial pterygoid is the principal tongue taste nerve. | Muscular action, sensory modality and nerve identity are separate questions. | 14,18,19
Mandibular protrusion | Forward movement assisted especially by bilateral lateral pterygoid activity and other contributors. | A student moves the lower jaw forward without opening widely. | Protrusion is the same as moving the jaw upward to close the mouth. | Multiple movement components can occur together but should be named separately. | 19
Mandibular retrusion | Backward movement with important posterior temporalis contribution and other assisting muscles. | A jaw returns posteriorly after being protruded. | Retrusion is produced only by the tongue's intrinsic transverse fibers. | Anatomical actions should be assigned to relevant jaw muscles, not nearby tongue structures. | 19
Mandibular elevation | Jaw-closing movement produced chiefly by masseter, temporalis and medial pterygoid. | Chewing brings lower teeth upward toward upper teeth. | Lateral pterygoid alone is the principal powerful elevator. | The three major elevators contrast with muscles assisting opening and translation. | 19,20
Mandibular depression | Jaw opening involves coordinated joint motion, suprahyoid assistance and other factors including gravity. | Opening the mouth occurs with a suitable stable hyoid arrangement. | Jaw opening is explained by one muscle acting independently of the hyoid or joint. | Opening combines rotation, translation and coordinated muscular activity. | 19,20,21
Suprahyoid assistance | Muscles such as digastric, mylohyoid and geniohyoid can assist jaw depression when the hyoid is stabilized. | A functional account explains why hyoid position matters during opening. | A suprahyoid muscle must move the mandible identically regardless of which attachment is stabilized. | Muscle effects depend on which related structures are fixed or moving. | 19,20
Side-to-side mandibular movement | Asymmetric coordinated activity of jaw muscles produces lateral excursions for chewing. | Alternating chewing movements are distinguished from symmetric forward protrusion. | Lateral excursion requires identical displacement of both condyles at all times. | The paired joints participate differently during lateral movement. | 14,19,20
Unilateral pterygoid action | Unopposed pterygoid activity generally moves the jaw toward the opposite side. | A teaching model relates left pterygoid activity to rightward excursion. | A unilateral pterygoid contraction must always move the jaw toward the contracting side. | Clinical movement patterns require the whole motor/joint context rather than one isolated rule. | 19,20
Mandibular nerve weakness | Weakness of one side's masticatory motor supply can produce deviation toward the weak side on opening. | In a simplified peripheral V3 teaching case, the jaw deviates toward the affected side. | Deviation alone identifies every possible cause of a jaw movement problem. | Joint restriction and other conditions can also alter movement; the rule requires stated peripheral weakness. | 13,19
TMJ dislocation principle | Excessive anterior displacement can place the condyle beyond the articular eminence and impair closure. | A case after wide opening illustrates an anatomical mechanism rather than a self-reduction instruction. | Every painless jaw click proves anterior dislocation. | Clicking and dislocation are different observations; diagnosis requires evaluation. | 21
Disc coordination | Normal jaw function depends on coordinated condyle, disc and surrounding tissues. | A learner distinguishes a mobile disc-condyle relationship from rigid fusion. | The disc must remain fixed to one point of temporal bone through all opening. | Functional relationships change during motion; symptoms cannot be inferred from one static drawing. | 6,7,21
Deep temporal arteries | Maxillary artery branches supplying temporalis regions. | A muscle-supply table pairs temporalis with deep temporal arteries. | Deep temporal arteries are the main vessels carrying taste from the tongue. | Arteries supply tissue; they do not conduct gustatory signals. | 14
Masseteric artery | A maxillary-system arterial branch contributing to masseter supply. | The masseteric neurovascular route is identified near the mandibular notch. | The masseteric artery is the palatine tonsil's sole nerve. | Keep blood supply, nerve supply and action columns distinct. | 14,15
Pterygoid arterial branches | Branches of the maxillary system contribute to pterygoid muscle supply. | A supply table pairs both pterygoid muscles with pterygoid arterial branches. | Both pterygoids depend exclusively on the anterior cerebral artery. | Head-and-neck muscular supply is regional rather than inferred from the cranial cavity nearby. | 14
Mastication and speech | Jaw stability and movement support chewing and contribute to positioning for articulation. | A student changes jaw position while forming a vowel without claiming the TMJ generates voiced sound. | The TMJ itself is the sound-generating vocal fold. | Articulatory support differs from laryngeal phonation; function involves a coordinated system. | 19,20
''')
add(5, '''
Pharynx | A mucosa-lined fibromuscular passage posterior to nasal cavity, mouth and larynx, continuing into the oesophagus. | A sagittal model traces food and air pathways through different pharyngeal levels. | The pharynx is the same structure as the trachea. | Pharyngeal and laryngeal passages are related but anatomically distinct. | 2,3
Pharyngeal extent | The pharynx extends from the skull base to the inferior cricoid border near C6 in the adult reference. | A learner places the pharyngoesophageal junction at the lower pharyngeal end. | The pharynx normally ends at the diaphragm's T10 hiatus. | C6, T10 and T11 are different landmarks; dimensions and exact positions vary. | 2,3,11
Pharyngeal wall layers | Mucosa, pharyngobasilar fascia, muscular layer and outer buccopharyngeal fascia are key layers. | A learner traces from the lumen outward through the four teaching layers. | Buccopharyngeal fascia is the innermost luminal epithelium. | Pharyngeal aponeurosis in the handout corresponds to the pharyngobasilar supporting layer. | 4
Pharyngobasilar fascia | A fibrous supporting layer internal to pharyngeal muscle, particularly important superiorly. | A wall diagram locates it between mucosa and muscular layer. | Pharyngobasilar fascia is a muscle that protrudes the tongue. | Fibrous support, muscle and mucosa perform different roles. | 4
Buccopharyngeal fascia | An outer fascial covering of the pharyngeal muscular wall. | A learner identifies it superficial to constrictor muscle in the teaching wall sequence. | It lies between taste buds and tongue epithelium. | Identify the organ and layer before assigning a functional role. | 4
Nasopharynx | The pharyngeal region behind the nasal cavities, extending from skull base to soft-palate level. | A diagram locates adenoids and the auditory-tube opening in this region. | The nasopharynx lies behind the larynx below the tongue base. | The name relates to nasal communication, not to every upper-airway structure. | 6,7,8
Oropharynx | The pharyngeal region posterior to the oral cavity, below the soft palate and above the lower pharyngeal boundary. | The palatine tonsils are located in its lateral walls. | The oropharynx is the air space within a frontal sinus. | Boundary conventions near the epiglottis/hyoid vary; identify the convention rather than demanding one universal line. | 6,7,9
Laryngopharynx | The lower pharyngeal region related to the posterior and lateral aspects of the larynx, continuing to the oesophagus. | A diagram shows both posterior and lateral channels beside the laryngeal inlet. | Laryngopharynx lies exclusively directly behind the larynx with no lateral component. | Hypopharynx is commonly used for this region, with clinical subregion conventions. | 6,7,11,14
Soft palate | A mobile posterior palatal structure helping separate nasopharyngeal and oral/pharyngeal pathways during function. | Soft-palate elevation contributes to closure during a swallow. | The soft palate is a rigid continuation of nasal bone. | Soft-palate motion and pharyngeal-wall participation matter together. | 8,9
Velopharyngeal closure | Functional separation involving the soft palate and pharyngeal walls that limits inappropriate oral-nasal communication. | A swallow example explains prevention of nasal escape of a bolus. | The hard palate alone changes shape to close the nasopharynx during swallowing. | Velopharyngeal is more precise than calling this closure the palatopharyngeal isthmus in every context. | 8
Auditory tube opening | The pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube lies in the lateral nasopharyngeal wall. | A nasopharyngeal model relates the opening to the middle-ear ventilation pathway. | The auditory tube normally opens into the inferior oesophagus. | A nasal-pharyngeal relation should not be confused with a nasal meatus opening. | 8,13
Pharyngeal tonsil | Lymphoid tissue in the roof/posterior wall region of the nasopharynx, commonly called the adenoid. | An image distinguishes adenoid tissue from palatine tonsils visible through the mouth. | Adenoids and palatine tonsils are identical structures at the same site. | Enlarged tissue may affect nearby pathways, but anatomy alone does not establish a diagnosis. | 8,13
Palatine tonsil | Lymphoid tissue in the lateral oropharyngeal region between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches. | A mouth diagram locates a tonsil between the anterior and posterior pillars. | A palatine tonsil lies inside the maxillary sinus. | Its position differs from pharyngeal and lingual tonsils. | 9,10
Palatoglossal arch | The anterior faucial pillar containing palatoglossus. | A student identifies the arch anterior to a palatine tonsil. | The anterior pillar contains stylopharyngeus as its defining muscle. | Palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal pillars are different boundaries. | 10
Palatopharyngeal arch | The posterior faucial pillar containing palatopharyngeus. | A diagram identifies it behind the palatine tonsil. | The posterior pillar is the same structure as the tongue's lingual frenulum. | Similar oral labels need their actual muscle and location specified. | 10
Pyriform recess | A lateral hypopharyngeal recess beside the laryngeal inlet. | A posterior view shows a recess on each side of the larynx. | A pyriform recess is one of the frontal sinuses. | Close nerve relations are clinically relevant, but a foreign-body diagram is not a self-removal guide. | 11,14
Postcricoid region | The hypopharyngeal region posterior to the cricoid-level laryngeal framework. | A transverse view distinguishes it from the paired lateral recesses. | Postcricoid means anterior to the external nasal cartilage. | Hypopharyngeal subregions help make localization more precise. | 14
Pharyngeal constrictors | Superior, middle and inferior muscles contributing to coordinated pharyngeal narrowing during swallowing. | A learner follows sequential pressure generation rather than treating the pharynx as a passive tube. | All pharyngeal muscles have only a tongue-protruding function. | Constrictors and longitudinal elevators have distinguishable predominant actions. | 4,19
Longitudinal pharyngeal muscles | Stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus contribute to elevation/shortening of the pharynx. | A swallowing explanation links elevation with coordinated constrictor activity. | These muscles are all parts of the nasal septal cartilage. | Muscle groups are organized by their attachments and actions, not name resemblance alone. | 4,19
Stylopharyngeus | A longitudinal pharyngeal muscle supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. | A nerve question identifies the major IX motor exception among pharyngeal muscles. | Stylopharyngeus receives its main motor supply from CN XII. | Its motor supply should not be confused with posterior-tongue sensory supply, which also involves IX. | 19
Pharyngeal motor supply | Most pharyngeal muscles receive vagal motor supply through pharyngeal pathways, with stylopharyngeus supplied by IX. | A learner states the usual X rule and the named IX exception. | Every muscle near the pharynx must receive motor supply exclusively from X. | Tensor veli palatini is a nearby palatal V3 exception, not one of the constrictors. | 19
Tensor veli palatini | A soft-palate muscle supplied by a V3 motor branch. | An exception table separates it from vagally supplied palatal muscles. | The tensor veli palatini is a taste receptor supplied only by VII. | Specify V3 rather than leaving the slide's broad V label undifferentiated. | 19
Pharyngeal sensory map | Major general sensory patterns are V2 for nasopharynx, IX for oropharynx and X for laryngopharynx. | A student aligns each region with the appropriate major cranial pathway. | Every pharyngeal region has identical general sensory supply from CN I. | This regional teaching map does not mean all mucosal territories have perfectly nonoverlapping boundaries. | 19
Pharyngeal plexus | A network including vagal, glossopharyngeal and sympathetic contributions around the pharyngeal wall. | A learner distinguishes a mixed network from one isolated motor nerve. | The pharyngeal plexus is exclusively a lymph node group. | Different components serve different modalities and functions. | 19
Gag reflex pathways | A conventional teaching model uses IX afferent and X efferent pathways. | A question distinguishes sensing a pharyngeal stimulus from the motor response. | A present gag reflex proves safe swallowing under every condition. | Reflex findings alone do not establish swallowing safety or exclude aspiration. | 19
Pharyngeal arterial supply | External-carotid branches including ascending pharyngeal and facial/maxillary/lingual contributions supply regions. | A vascular tree is traced through parent vessels rather than memorized as unrelated names. | The pharynx receives all blood directly from one coronary artery. | Contributions vary by region and collateral routes. | 15
Ascending pharyngeal artery | An external carotid branch contributing to pharyngeal supply. | A student distinguishes it from the ascending palatine branch of the facial artery. | Ascending pharyngeal and ascending palatine are identical names for one branch. | Similar directional names do not establish the same parent vessel. | 15
Tonsillar facial branch | A facial artery branch contributing importantly to palatine tonsillar supply. | A tonsillar supply question follows the branch back to facial and external carotid arteries. | The tonsillar branch in this context originates from the ophthalmic artery. | The palatine tonsil has several contributing arteries; one major branch is not necessarily exclusive. | 15
Ascending palatine artery | A facial artery branch contributing to palatal/pharyngeal regions. | It is distinguished from the maxillary-system descending palatine artery. | Ascending and descending palatine arteries both necessarily arise from the same named parent. | Parent-vessel distinctions are a common anatomy examination target. | 15
Descending palatine pathway | A maxillary arterial route contributing through palatine branches. | A learner traces the descending palatine branch from the maxillary rather than facial artery. | The descending palatine artery is a motor branch of V3. | Artery names, cranial nerves and muscle actions belong to different categories. | 15
Dorsal lingual branches | Lingual artery branches contributing to posterior tongue and neighboring regions. | A regional supply diagram includes them near the tongue base. | Dorsal lingual branches are branches of the hypoglossal nerve. | Blood vessels and nerves may run near each other without being the same structure. | 15
Nasopharyngeal lymph drainage | Important routes include retropharyngeal and upper deep cervical nodes, commonly including the level II region. | A diagram follows posterior nasopharyngeal drainage toward deep cervical chains. | Nasopharyngeal lymph drains exclusively to superficial inguinal nodes. | Regional routes can be bilateral and overlapping rather than one fixed isolated channel. | 16
Oropharyngeal lymph drainage | Deep cervical drainage includes the jugulodigastric region associated with tonsillar drainage. | A learner associates a tonsillar region with upper deep cervical nodes. | Lymph drainage and tonsillar arterial supply have identical named endpoints. | Lymphatic nodes and arterial branches must be learned separately. | 17
Hypopharyngeal lymph drainage | Deep cervical pathways, often described across levels II-IV with possible paratracheal contributions, receive hypopharyngeal drainage. | A map shows several levels rather than a single exclusive node. | A hypopharyngeal region can drain only to an axillary node. | Patterns depend on subsite and are not sufficient alone to infer disease spread in an individual. | 18
Sympathetic pharyngeal contribution | Cervical sympathetic pathways, including superior cervical ganglion-related fibers, contribute autonomic supply. | A chart separates autonomic contributions from IX/X motor and sensory roles. | Sympathetic fibers are the main voluntary motor supply to stylopharyngeus. | Vasomotor/autonomic and skeletal motor functions are distinct. | 19
Parasympathetic regional qualification | Secretomotor pathways differ by gland and region; a single VII label does not explain every pharyngeal autonomic function. | A learner treats the slide's parasympathetic shorthand as incomplete rather than universal. | All autonomic activity throughout the pharynx is carried exclusively by VII. | Specify the target tissue and pathway when more detailed autonomic anatomy is required. | 19
Hoarseness | A perceptual voice change associated with altered laryngeal/phonatory function and many possible causes. | A lecture's hoarseness list is kept separate from the pharyngeal wall anatomy. | A hoarse voice alone diagnoses pharyngeal cancer. | The pharynx and larynx are related, but a symptom is not a specific anatomical diagnosis. | 5
Benign vocal-fold lesions | Nodules, many polyps and other lesions can affect vibration without being equivalent to malignancy. | A learner corrects a list that groups every polyp or nodule as cancer. | Every vocal nodule is a malignant neoplasm. | The broad slide category is misleading; lesion type needs clinical evaluation. | 5
Laryngeal nerve considerations | Recurrent and superior laryngeal pathways affect different components of laryngeal sensation and movement. | A learner distinguishes pharyngeal sensory maps from laryngeal nerve injury mechanisms. | Every hoarse voice is caused by the same isolated nerve lesion. | Inflammation, structural lesions, neurological conditions and other causes remain possible. | 5
Voice differential categories | Congenital, inflammatory, structural, neurological and other factors can contribute to dysphonia. | A teacher's persistent voice change prompts appropriate evaluation rather than diagnosis from a list. | Reflux must be the cause of every persistent voice symptom. | The handout lists possibilities, not proof of causation or a treatment prescription. | 5
Indirect examination principles | Mirrors or endoscopes can visualize selected upper-airway regions through clinically appropriate techniques. | Indirect laryngoscopy and posterior rhinoscopy are identified as examination approaches in images. | A static diagram replaces a trained clinical examination of airway symptoms. | These are recognition concepts; the website does not teach invasive self-examination. | 12,13
''')
add(6, '''
Oral cavity regions | The mouth includes a vestibule and oral cavity proper with lips, cheeks, teeth, palate, floor and tongue-related boundaries. | A learner distinguishes the space outside the teeth from the space within the dental arches. | Oral vestibule and nasal vestibule are the same anatomical space. | Similar terms need their organ and boundaries specified. | 2
Hard palate | The bony anterior roof of the mouth and corresponding floor of the nasal cavities. | A model locates it anterior to the mobile soft palate. | The hard palate actively elevates to seal the nasopharynx during swallowing. | Mobility and closure principally involve soft-palate/pharyngeal structures, not bending of bone. | 2
Lingual frenulum | A mucosal fold connecting the tongue's undersurface with the oral floor region. | A mouth diagram identifies the fold beneath a raised tongue. | The lingual frenulum is the V-shaped sulcus on the tongue's dorsum. | A visible fold and a dorsal groove are distinct landmarks; functional significance needs assessment. | 2
Tongue | A highly mobile muscular organ contributing to bolus control, taste, articulation and airway-related function. | A learner describes both shaping for speech and moving food during chewing. | The tongue is supported by a rigid internal bony skeleton. | Muscular organization and external attachments allow flexible shape and position changes. | 3,4,5,16
Tongue oral part | The anterior two-thirds of the tongue, anterior to the sulcus terminalis. | A dorsal view separates the oral part from the posterior pharyngeal part. | The entire tongue anterior to the epiglottis belongs to the same sensory territory. | Regional boundaries and nerve exceptions matter; surface continuity does not mean uniform supply. | 5
Tongue pharyngeal part | The posterior third of the tongue, facing the oropharynx and associated with lingual lymphoid tissue. | A model locates tongue-base tissue behind the terminal sulcus. | The pharyngeal part is the tongue's free anterior tip. | The posterior region has different principal sensory supply from most anterior mucosa. | 5
Sulcus terminalis | A V-shaped groove marking the surface boundary between oral and pharyngeal tongue parts. | A dorsal diagram locates vallate papillae just anterior to the V-shaped boundary. | The sulcus terminalis is the tongue's main artery. | Landmarks, vessels and muscles should not be confused. | 5
Foramen cecum | A small midline landmark at the apex of the terminal sulcus associated with thyroglossal development. | A developmental question locates the thyroid-origin landmark on the tongue. | The foramen cecum is the opening of the parotid duct in the cheek. | It is a developmental landmark, not a normal open passage through which food travels. | 5
Tongue developmental regions | Tongue mucosal regions reflect first, third and fourth arch contributions, with second-arch tissue largely overgrown. | A learner relates anterior general sensation to V3 and posterior general/taste supply to IX. | The first four arches each contribute equally to every adult tongue region. | The handout's first-to-fourth shorthand should not imply equal persistent contributions; most tongue muscle derives from occipital myotomes. | 4,14
Extrinsic tongue muscles | Muscles with attachments outside the tongue that chiefly change its position, while also influencing shape. | Genioglossus and styloglossus are contrasted with intrinsic longitudinal muscles. | Extrinsic means a tongue muscle has no attachment outside the tongue. | Position versus shape is a useful distinction, not an absolute functional separation. | 6,7
Intrinsic tongue muscles | Muscles located within the tongue that chiefly alter its shape. | A learner groups superior/inferior longitudinal, transverse and vertical fibers. | Intrinsic tongue muscles all begin on the zygomatic arch. | All four intrinsic groups receive hypoglossal motor supply in the usual teaching pattern. | 8,9
Genioglossus | An extrinsic tongue muscle arising from the superior mental spine region of the mandible and spreading into tongue/hyoid regions. | Bilateral activity is identified as an important contributor to protrusion. | Genioglossus is the main muscle retracting the mandible at the TMJ. | Tongue and jaw actions must be separated despite a mandibular attachment. | 6,7
Genioglossus action | Fiber groups contribute to protrusion and depression of the tongue, with asymmetric activity affecting direction. | A student distinguishes protruding the tongue from protruding the jaw. | Every genioglossus fiber produces exactly the same movement in every task. | Distributed fiber actions and coordination make one-word summaries incomplete. | 6,7
Hyoglossus | An extrinsic tongue muscle extending from the hyoid toward the side of the tongue, contributing to depression. | A side view connects the hyoid with the tongue's lateral region. | Hyoglossus originates principally on the nasal septum. | Its name and attachments identify its hyoid-tongue relationship. | 6,7
Styloglossus | An extrinsic muscle extending from the styloid region toward the tongue and contributing to retraction/elevation. | A learner follows fibers downward and forward toward the tongue's side. | Styloglossus is supplied principally by the facial nerve for ordinary motor function. | Its usual motor supply is XII, unlike palatoglossus. | 6,7
Palatoglossus | A muscle from the soft palate to the tongue, contributing to tongue-root elevation and narrowing of the oral-pharyngeal opening. | The anterior faucial pillar is related to its course. | Palatoglossus follows the usual XII motor rule for all tongue muscles without exception. | The usual teaching exception is vagal motor supply through the pharyngeal plexus; separate XI supply is not the preferred modern shorthand. | 6
Superior longitudinal tongue muscle | An intrinsic fiber group contributing to shortening and upward curling of tongue margins/tip. | A learner distinguishes curling the tip upward from moving the whole tongue backward. | The superior longitudinal muscle is a rigid ligament with no motor role. | Coordinated fibers shape the tongue; isolated action descriptions are introductory simplifications. | 8,9
Inferior longitudinal tongue muscle | An intrinsic fiber group contributing to shortening and downward curling of the tongue tip. | A comparison pairs superior and inferior longitudinal fibers with different tip-shaping effects. | Inferior longitudinal means the muscle lies outside the tongue and arises on the skull. | It is intrinsic, despite its connections with surrounding fiber systems. | 8,9
Transverse tongue muscle | Intrinsic fibers that narrow and elongate the tongue. | A shape task makes the tongue narrower rather than treating the fibers as a dividing wall. | The transverse muscle is the median connective-tissue septum that divides the tongue. | Muscle fibers and the lingual septum are different structures; the slide's wording needs clarification. | 8,9
Vertical tongue muscle | Intrinsic fibers contributing to flattening and broadening of the tongue. | A learner contrasts a flatter broad shape with the narrow elongated effect of transverse fibers. | Vertical fibers primarily turn the mandible around its condyle. | The target is tongue shape, not jaw-joint rotation. | 8,9
Median lingual septum | A connective-tissue partition associated with the tongue's midline organization. | A section labels the septum separately from transverse muscle fibers. | The median septum is an artery supplying the tongue tip. | Connective tissue, muscle and vessels require separate labels. | 9
Hypoglossal motor rule | CN XII supplies intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles, with palatoglossus as the usual vagal exception. | A motor table separates XII tongue muscles from the V3 masticatory group. | CN VII is the principal voluntary motor nerve to all tongue muscles. | Taste, general sensation and motor supply must be learned as separate maps. | 6,14,15
Peripheral XII lesion pattern | A unilateral lower motor neuron hypoglossal lesion can produce ipsilateral weakness and deviation toward the affected side on protrusion. | In a stated right peripheral XII case, the tongue deviates right when protruded. | The same side rule localizes every central and peripheral tongue deviation without qualification. | Upper motor neuron patterns and other causes require different context; the simplified rule needs the stated lesion type. | 6,15
Filiform papillae | Numerous mechanical papillae without taste buds in the standard teaching classification. | A learner distinguishes their frictional role from gustatory papillae. | Filiform papillae contain the majority of tongue taste buds. | Papillae are surface structures; taste buds are sensory organs within appropriate epithelia. | 10,11
Fungiform papillae | Mushroom-shaped papillae, particularly evident anteriorly, associated with taste buds. | A tongue diagram contrasts scattered fungiform with a posterior V-shaped vallate row. | Fungiform papillae are the same as the tongue's intrinsic longitudinal muscles. | Shape/location of papillae differs from underlying muscular organization. | 10,11
Circumvallate papillae | Large papillae arranged near and just anterior to the terminal sulcus, with glossopharyngeal taste innervation. | A student recognizes the IX exception despite their position in the oral tongue. | All taste anterior to the terminal sulcus is carried by VII without exception. | Vallate papillae are a key regional nerve exception. | 10,11,14,15
Foliate papillae | Leaf-like folds along posterolateral tongue margins associated with taste buds. | A lateral tongue image shows folds rather than a midline V-shaped row. | Foliate papillae are located inside the nasal inferior meatus. | Location and morphology differentiate the four teaching papilla types. | 10,11
Taste bud | A sensory epithelial structure containing cells involved in gustatory transduction. | A learner distinguishes a taste bud from the larger surface papilla supporting it. | A taste bud and an entire papilla are necessarily identical structures. | Taste buds also occur in selected extra-lingual sites; they are not simply every visible tongue bump. | 10,11,16
Taste-map qualification | Taste qualities are not confined to separate exclusive sweet, sour, salty or bitter zones of the tongue. | A learner rejects a rigid zone map while recognizing regional sensitivity differences. | Sweet can be detected only by the tongue tip and nowhere else. | Regional variation does not justify mutually exclusive taste territories. | 10,14,15,16
Lingual artery | A major external-carotid branch supplying the tongue through several branches. | A vascular tree traces lingual supply from the external rather than internal carotid artery. | Lingual artery is a branch of the mandibular nerve. | Similar neighboring names do not make arteries and nerves equivalent. | 12,13
Dorsal lingual supply | Lingual artery branches contribute to posterior tongue regions. | A dorsal diagram relates these branches to the tongue base. | Dorsal lingual branches carry voluntary motor commands from the brainstem. | Vessels deliver blood; motor commands follow nerves. | 12,13
Deep lingual artery | A lingual arterial branch running toward the anterior tongue. | A student follows the artery toward the tongue tip on a supply diagram. | Deep lingual artery is the motor exception supplying palatoglossus. | A blood-supply branch is not a cranial motor nerve. | 12,13
Lingual venous drainage | Tongue veins ultimately communicate with internal-jugular-related venous drainage. | A vascular figure distinguishes blue venous channels from arterial branches. | Tongue venous blood drains first into a palatine tonsil lymph node. | Venous return and lymphatic drainage are different systems. | 13
Tongue lymph drainage | Tip, lateral oral regions, central regions and posterior tongue have different routes with important crossover. | A learner notes that central/posterior drainage can be bilateral rather than strictly unilateral. | Every tongue region drains to one node on only the same side. | Submental, submandibular and deep cervical pathways vary by region; real spread is assessed clinically. | 12,13
Anterior tongue general sensation | The lingual nerve from V3 carries general sensation from most anterior two-thirds mucosa. | Touch is traced separately from taste in an anterior tongue question. | The chorda tympani is the sole nerve for all ordinary touch from the anterior tongue. | A named region does not answer the nerve question until the sensory modality is specified. | 14,15
Anterior tongue taste | Chorda tympani fibers from VII travel with the lingual nerve to carry taste from most anterior tongue regions. | A student distinguishes a carrier nerve from the cranial origin of taste fibers. | Taste fibers become trigeminal-origin fibers merely because they travel in the lingual nerve. | Traveling together does not change the fibers' cranial origin; vallate papillae are an IX exception. | 14,15
Posterior tongue sensation | IX carries both general sensation and taste from much of the posterior third. | A region-modality table pairs posterior tongue touch and taste with glossopharyngeal pathways. | Posterior tongue taste is always supplied by the external laryngeal motor branch. | The posterior tongue differs from epiglottic-adjacent vagal sensory territory. | 14,15
Epiglottic-adjacent tongue sensation | Internal laryngeal vagal pathways contribute general and taste sensation near the epiglottic region. | A map labels the most posterior sensory area separately from most posterior-third IX territory. | CN XII carries taste from the epiglottic region. | Hypoglossal is primarily a somatic motor pathway, not the tongue taste nerve. | 14,15
Chorda tympani functions | A VII branch carries taste fibers and preganglionic secretomotor fibers toward submandibular-ganglion pathways. | Taste loss is distinguished from changes in salivary pathways in a conceptual nerve question. | Chorda tympani supplies the motor power of all intrinsic tongue muscles. | Multiple fiber types can share a route without supplying every tongue function. | 14,15
Tongue and articulation | Tongue position and shape help create oral constrictions and resonance patterns for speech. | A Lahore student compares tongue contact in familiar speech sounds without assuming a single muscle works alone. | One visible tongue shape identifies the activity of exactly one muscle in isolation. | Speech uses coordinated muscular patterns and sensory feedback. | 16
Bolus manipulation | Tongue movements gather, position and move food during oral processing. | A familiar meal example distinguishes gathering food from triggering an entire swallow. | Taste perception alone propels a bolus mechanically into the stomach. | Sensory and motor processes cooperate but are not the same function. | 4,16,19
Tongue airway relationship | Tongue position can influence upper-airway patency even though the organ is attached and cannot be literally swallowed whole. | A learner separates the myth of swallowing the tongue from possible posterior airway obstruction. | Attachment means tongue position can never contribute to airway obstruction. | Airway concerns are real and need appropriate care; the anatomical myth and the mechanism must be distinguished. | 4
Oesophagus | A muscular tube conveying swallowed material from pharynx toward stomach. | A sagittal model distinguishes it from the airway anteriorly. | The oesophagus is the main conducting airway between larynx and lung. | Esophagus and oesophagus are spelling variants of the same organ. | 17
Oesophageal beginning | The adult oesophagus begins near the lower cricoid border at approximately C6. | A learner links this level to the pharyngoesophageal transition. | The oesophagus begins at the T10 diaphragmatic hiatus. | C6 marks the beginning; T10 describes the hiatus rather than the entire organ's origin. | 17,18
Oesophageal hiatus | The oesophagus passes through the diaphragm near T10 in the standard reference. | A landmark sequence separates C6 beginning, T10 hiatus and approximately T11 gastric entry. | T10 and T11 must refer to the identical boundary of the entire oesophagus. | Reference levels are useful approximations influenced by posture and individual anatomy. | 17,18
Oesophageal termination | The oesophagus joins the stomach near the cardia, commonly referenced around T11. | The short abdominal segment is placed between diaphragm and gastric entry. | The oesophagus ends at the laryngeal inlet. | The gastric junction differs from the upper pharyngoesophageal junction. | 17
Oesophageal length and parts | The adult organ is approximately 25 cm long and has cervical, thoracic and short abdominal parts. | A learner treats listed part lengths as approximations rather than an exact total for every adult. | Every person's oesophagus has exactly the same length regardless of body size. | Adult reference dimensions vary; clinical measurement uses the actual person and context. | 17
Cervical oesophageal relations | The cervical oesophagus lies posterior to the trachea and anterior to the vertebral region. | A direction question compares the food passage with the airway. | The cervical oesophagus lies anterior to the trachea throughout its course. | This relation connects anatomy with the standard anterior-posterior vocabulary. | 17
Thoracic oesophageal relations | The oesophagus passes through superior/posterior mediastinal regions with important aortic, bronchial and cardiac relations. | A learner locates it posterior to the left atrial region. | The entire thoracic oesophagus lies inside the nasal cavity. | Relations vary along its course rather than one neighbor applying identically at all levels. | 17
Upper oesophageal constriction | The pharyngoesophageal junction forms a normal upper narrowing near C6. | A constriction list starts at the cricoid-level junction rather than the stomach. | Every normal constriction is evidence of a tumor. | Normal anatomical narrowings differ from pathological strictures. | 18
Aortic and bronchial impressions | The aortic arch and left main bronchus contribute middle oesophageal narrowings. | A learner orders the aortic impression before the left bronchial impression along the descending route. | The handout's T3/T6 labels are immutable levels in every adult. | The aortic relation is commonly placed around T4-T5; the left bronchial relation is inferior and varies by convention. Sources may group these as one broncho-aortic constriction; prioritize structures and sequence. | 18
Diaphragmatic oesophageal narrowing | Passage through the diaphragm contributes a normal lower constriction near T10. | A diagram links the lowest listed normal constriction with the hiatus. | The diaphragmatic constriction is the same anatomical site as the C6 beginning. | Three-versus-four constriction schemes may group middle impressions differently. | 18
Incisor-distance qualification | Clinical distance estimates to oesophageal landmarks are approximate and depend on technique and individual anatomy. | A learner uses roughly 15 cm to the upper junction as a teaching estimate rather than a universal measurement. | A slide's distance from incisors is an exact measurement for every person. | Vertebral level, organ length and endoscopic distance are different reference quantities. | 18
Oesophageal muscle distribution | Proximal muscle is predominantly striated, distal predominantly smooth, with an intervening transition. | A learner explains why the oesophagus is not simply one uniform skeletal muscle tube. | Oesophageal wall muscle is identical in type throughout the entire organ. | Transitions are gradual; an exact equal-third boundary should not be assumed for every person. | 17,19
Peristalsis | Coordinated propagating muscular activity that moves contents along a tubular organ. | A swallow explanation includes oesophageal transport even when gravity alone would not suffice. | Food reaches the stomach solely because it falls under gravity. | Gravity can assist in some postures, but active muscular transport is central. | 19,20
Upper oesophageal sphincter | A functional high-pressure segment involving cricopharyngeal and neighboring structures at the pharyngoesophageal transition. | A learner relates passage opening to relaxation, traction and bolus forces. | The UES is simply the vocal folds closing during speech. | Sphincter function and airway closure are distinct coordinated components. | 19
Lower oesophageal sphincter | A physiological high-pressure region at the lower oesophageal junction supported by surrounding anatomy. | An explanation distinguishes lower barrier function from upper sphincter opening. | The LES must be a separately visible rigid cartilaginous ring. | Functional sphincters need not appear as a discrete anatomical ring. | 17,19
Oral preparatory phase | Food is processed, mixed with saliva and organized before posterior oral transport. | A meal example describes chewing and gathering a bolus. | Oral preparation is the same event as oesophageal peristalsis. | Chewing/processing and subsequent transport can overlap during natural eating. | 19,20
Oral propulsive phase | Tongue-palate activity moves a prepared bolus posteriorly toward the pharynx. | A student separates bolus propulsion from merely recognizing a food's taste. | The tongue contributes no force to oral bolus transport. | The oral stage includes coordinated containment and propulsion rather than one isolated movement. | 19,20
Pharyngeal swallow | A coordinated stage involving bolus propulsion, airway protection and opening of the pharyngoesophageal passage. | A learner lists several coupled events rather than one single protective flap. | Pharyngeal swallowing consists only of epiglottic motion. | The stage includes neuromuscular coordination across multiple structures. | 19,20
Hyolaryngeal excursion | Superior/anterior movement of the hyoid-laryngeal complex during swallowing. | An explanation connects this excursion with airway protection and assisted UES opening. | Hyolaryngeal excursion means the tongue alone becomes narrower. | Movement of the complex differs from intrinsic tongue-shape change. | 19
Airway protection in swallowing | Multiple coordinated events limit entry into the laryngeal airway. | A diagram relates laryngeal closure, hyolaryngeal movement and epiglottic inversion. | The epiglottis alone guarantees that aspiration cannot occur. | No single pictured component proves swallowing safety. | 19,20
Epiglottic inversion | Movement of the epiglottis during swallowing associated with surrounding pressures and structural motion. | A student identifies inversion as part of a coordinated protective sequence. | The epiglottis is the only muscle contracting to pull food into the stomach. | The epiglottis is elastic cartilage; its movement is not an isolated muscle action. | 19,20
Pharyngoesophageal segment opening | Opening during swallowing depends on reduced sphincter resistance, hyolaryngeal traction and bolus-related forces. | A learner explains why relaxation alone is not the complete mechanical account. | The segment opens only because a rigid lid detaches from the oesophagus. | Several neural and mechanical processes cooperate. | 19
Oesophageal phase | Bolus transport toward the stomach through coordinated oesophageal activity. | A student distinguishes this phase from oral preparation and pharyngeal airway closure. | A successful oral swallow proves that all oesophageal transport is normal. | Different stages require appropriate evaluation; success at one stage does not establish all others. | 19,20
Penetration versus aspiration | Penetration enters the laryngeal vestibule without passing below the vocal folds; aspiration passes below them. | A terminology exercise uses the vocal-fold level as the defining boundary. | Aspiration and nasal regurgitation are identical events. | Airway entry and escape toward the nose involve different routes. | 19,20
Nasal regurgitation | Escape of swallowed material toward or through the nasal passage. | An educational case links incomplete velopharyngeal separation with nasal escape without diagnosing its cause. | Nasal regurgitation proves that food has passed below the vocal folds. | Direction of escape distinguishes it from aspiration. | 19
Swallow phase-model qualification | Three-stage descriptions often combine oral preparation and propulsion, while four-stage schemes separate them. | A student reconciles the slide's three main columns with two oral subphases. | Three-stage and four-stage schemes necessarily describe incompatible anatomy. | State the model and acknowledge overlap and coordination during natural eating. | 19,20
''')
add(3, '''
Lateral nasal wall bones | Contributions include maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, inferior concha, palatine and sphenoid regions. | A diagram labels several bones rather than assigning the whole lateral wall to one concha. | All lateral-wall bones are components of the mandibular ramus. | A wall is assembled from several structures; projections and passages are not the entire wall. | 2
Nasal secretomotor pathway | Parasympathetic fibers reach pterygopalatine-ganglion pathways through facial greater-petrosal contributions and then travel with regional branches. | A learner separates a V2 carrier route from the origin of secretomotor fibers. | Every fiber traveling with V2 must be a trigeminal-origin somatic sensory fiber. | Fiber origin, ganglion and final carrier should be distinguished; this regional route does not explain every pharyngeal gland. | 4
Systemic inflammatory differential | The handout lists conditions such as GPA, tuberculosis, syphilis, polyarteritis nodosa and SLE among systemic considerations. | A learner recognizes a differential list without diagnosing one condition from ordinary nasal irritation. | Every person with epistaxis has a systemic vasculitic disorder. | These are possible contexts requiring clinical evidence, not equal-frequency causes or age-based diagnoses. | 17
Bleeding-category qualification | Broad bleeding lists may mix inherited factor disorders, platelet problems, acquired disease and myeloproliferative conditions. | Polycythemia vera is recognized as a myeloproliferative disorder rather than simply inherited factor deficiency. | Every item under primary coagulopathy in the slide has the same inherited mechanism. | Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease, thrombocytopenia and polycythemia vera are not interchangeable diagnoses. | 18
''')
add(6, '''
Geniohyoid distinction | A suprahyoid muscle linking the mandibular mental-spine region to the hyoid, distinct from genioglossus. | A diagram labels geniohyoid below the tongue-related genioglossus. | Geniohyoid is one of the four intrinsic tongue muscles. | C1 fibers traveling with XII supply geniohyoid; a carrier nerve does not change the spinal origin of those fibers. | 7
''')
